  "headline": {
    "ranking_by_mae": [
      "quantum",
      "learned",
      "reference"
    ],
    "mae_quantum": 2.0978084714548806,
    "mae_learned": 2.410699815837938,
    "mae_reference": 5.48596685082873,
    "quantum_vs_learned_verdict": "NOT DISTINGUISHABLE on the typical observation: median |err| difference +0.020 kcal/mol, Wilcoxon p=0.553, sign test p=0.108, Cliff's delta -0.042. The mean difference is small but formally significant (-0.313 kcal/mol, 95% CI -0.471 to -0.165, paired t p=5.4e-5) and is carried by the held-out molecules and the weak-bond tail, where quantum is clearly better.",
    "quantum_vs_reference_verdict": "quantum clearly better; mean difference -3.388 kcal/mol, all tests p<1e-58, Cliff's delta -0.631",
    "learned_vs_reference_verdict": "learned clearly better; mean difference -3.075 kcal/mol, all tests p<1e-49, Cliff's delta -0.573",
    "calibration_verdict": "quantum (slope 0.932) and learned (slope 0.891) are compressed toward the mean - they overestimate weak bonds and slightly underestimate strong ones; reference (slope 1.073) is amplified - it overestimates strong bonds. Compression means the calibrated channels lose accuracy at the low end of the experimental range (MAE 3.75 / 5.37 kcal/mol in the weakest decile vs 1.89 / 2.06 in the middle 80%), not at the high end.",
    "training_split_verdict": "All three channels are less accurate on the 212 held-out molecules, but the decay is NOT uniform and is partly a composition/extrapolation artefact. Aggregate MAE: quantum 1.87 -> 2.45 (+0.58, Welch p=0.005), learned 1.81 -> 3.35 (+1.55, p=7e-9), reference 5.02 -> 6.21 (+1.18, p=0.001). Stratified by bond class the picture splits: on C-H, quantum shows no degradation at all (1.95 -> 2.03, p=0.70) and reference none (5.71 -> 5.72, p=0.98), while learned still degrades sharply (1.95 -> 3.08, p=4e-5); on C-C all three degrade (quantum 1.81 -> 3.73, learned 1.70 -> 4.15, reference 4.50 -> 7.78). Only 4 of the 212 held-out rows lie outside the training experimental range (61.4-160.2 kcal/mol) and they are wildly wrong for all three channels (MAE 11.3-15.1 kcal/mol), but they account for only ~29% of quantum's, ~15% of learned's and ~9% of reference's aggregate degradation. So: learned's out-of-sample decay is robust to every control; quantum's and reference's aggregate decay is largely, though not entirely, a bond-class composition effect (the held-out set is 75% C-H while the training set is 56% C-C).",
    "correlation_verdict": "quantum and learned errors are strongly coupled (|err| Pearson 0.754, sign agreement 0.866); reference errors are nearly independent of both (|err| Pearson 0.214 / 0.196, sign agreement 0.545 / 0.560, partial correlations ~0.05-0.10). Reference fails largely where the others succeed - it is a genuinely different estimator, not a harder version of the same one."
  },
